### Problem Description

We use remote source maps (e.g. `//# sourceMappingURL=https://something-deployed/index.js.map`), in electron's main and utility processes. Historically, remote source maps for node.js applications could not be resolved by Chrome DevTools.

Now the situation has improved - in Node.js 22.19 they added `--experimental-inspector-network-resource` flag, which allows applications to manually add [support for remote source map resolution](https://nodejs.org/api/inspector.html#inspectornetworkresourcesput)

E.g. Imagine i have the following script:
```
import inspector from 'node:inspector';

async function setNetworkResources() {
  const mapUrl = 'http://localhost:4000/index.js.map';
  const distAppJsMap = await fetch(mapUrl).then((res) => res.text());
  inspector.NetworkResources.put(mapUrl, distAppJsMap);
  // TS source is included in the js.map, so we can skip the .ts fetch
};

setNetworkResources().then(() => {
  setInterval(() => {
    console.log("ping!")
  }, 5000)
}); 
```
If I compile the code and generate source maps, then run  `node --inspect=9230 --experimental-inspector-network-resource index.js`, I can use Chromes 'dedicated DevTools for Node', and see that it successfully resolves source maps, allowing me to debug the original TS code.

<img width="360" height="182" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/13b96832-7441-4836-a518-59e03f4e7e3b" />

However, the same `setNetworkResources` doesn't work in electron, because it only supports a subset of Node.js flags.

### Proposed Solution

Add support for `--experimental-inspector-network-resource` CLI flags for both main and utility processes.

### Alternatives Considered

The only 'real' alternative is not to have source map resolution for main/utility processes in Chrome inspector (which is the current situation). It's not ideal, because there are developers who prefer using Chrome's inspector over VS Code


### Additional Information

Long term, consider implementing `setNetworkResources` directly in electron (to provide remote source map resolution for all applications "out of the box") - I know it's a long shot, but I do think it might be useful for for other electron applications as well

